License boundaries in this repository
=====================================

This repository is MIT-licensed overall (see LICENSE), except:

* pkgs/kernel/patches/*.patch are Linux kernel patches, licensed GPL-2.0-only.
  Each patch file's header records its provenance; those headers are
  preserved verbatim.
* pkgs/bootimg/dts/liuqin-abl-boot-overlay.dts is GPL-2.0-only (device-tree source,
  carried unchanged from the downstream project).
* pkgs/power-keyd/liuqin-power-keyd.c, liuqin-power-key-action.sh
  and liuqin-power-menu.py are MIT, carried from the downstream project
  (yzddmr6/xiaomipad-6pro-mainline).
* pkgs/alsa-ucm/ucm2/ (ALSA UCM2 configuration) is BSD-3-Clause per upstream
  alsa-ucm-conf; the liuqin card files originate from the downstream
  project.
* pkgs/fingerprint/fpc-oem-src/ is the vendored source of the OEM FPC1264
  userspace component: originally yzddmr6/xiaomipad-6pro-mainline PR #11,
  head 17c534bdc41cc7ce76d402990530809978069b73 of the yuzelingsha fork, and
  byte-identical to that revision's sparse checkout of
  device/fingerprint/oem/src/fingerprint/{oem,libfprint-tod,fprintd-oem}.  Its
  file headers carry the licenses: BSD-2-Clause for the QSEE/Keymaster
  clients, LGPL-2.1-or-later for the libfprint TOD driver, MIT for the Python
  entry points, GPL-2.0-or-later for the fprintd include
  (fprintd-oem/oem-update.inc); the remaining data/prose files (README.md,
  *.conf, the pam.d entry, the fprintd patch) carry no header and follow the
  component's terms.  It is vendored rather than fetched so the tree does not
  depend on an unmerged PR branch; re-sync with
  `git sparse-checkout set device/fingerprint/oem/src/fingerprint/{oem,libfprint-tod,fprintd-oem}`
  at that revision.
* pkgs/hexagonrpc/patches/*.patch and pkgs/iio-sensor-proxy/patches/*.patch
  are patches to hexagonrpc (GPL-2.0) and iio-sensor-proxy (GPL-3.0 / LGPL);
  they retain their downstream headers.
* pkgs/firmware.nix, pkgs/sensors-config.nix and pkgs/bootimg/stock-*.nix
  reference proprietary Qualcomm/Xiaomi binaries that are NOT redistributable.
  They are consumed as `requireFile` inputs supplied by the operator: the
  archives are neither committed nor part of the flake source, and the tree
  itself carries no vendor binary. That includes the downstream v0.3.1
  release image whose ramdisk carries the bulk firmware tree - it is an
  operator input too, so no build reaches for a third-party release asset.
  The data/*.tar.zst working copies, data/release-v0.3.1/boot.img and the
  two stock DT archives are listed in .gitignore.
  History note: stock-dtbo-entries.tar.zst and stock-base-dtbs.tar.zst were
  tracked in earlier commits; those commits still contain them. Publishing
  from that history needs a rewrite
  (`git filter-repo --path-glob 'data/*.tar.zst'
    --path-glob 'pkgs/bootimg/stock-*.tar.zst' --invert-paths`)
  or a fresh root commit.

Vendor-derived data in the device patches
-----------------------------------------

The camera patches embed register tables decoded from Xiaomi/Qualcomm binaries
and fall under the same care as the firmware above; none of the source
containers (com.qti.sensormodule.*.bin, the vendor camera.ko) is committed.

* pkgs/kernel/patches/0012-liuqin-camera-core.patch adds
  drivers/media/i2c/qcom-liuqin-sensors.c with the IMX596 and SC202CS init and
  mode tables, and the CSIPHY 2.1.3 lane table in
  camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c; the tables were machine-generated during bring-up
  (the generator lived in the bring-up branch).
* pkgs/kernel/patches/0013-liuqin-camera-tuning.patch carries the S5KJN1
  vendor mode tables.

Decision (2026-10-07): these tables stay in the tracked tree. They are
register values - interface data the mainline drivers need compiled in to
drive the sensors - not vendor binaries, and the port cannot work without
them. If that posture ever changes, the move is to extract the table hunks
into an operator-supplied payload (a patch fragment consumed through
requireFile) that the kernel build applies after the code patch; that split
requires a real kernel build to validate and would make camera support depend
on the operator's payload.

Knowledge (partition geometry, ABL behavior, systemd ordering rationale)
was extracted from the downstream project and re-expressed in Nix; no
downstream shell scripts are used in the build chain.
